   "moduleId": "org-complexity",
   "question": "A company is in the process of implementing AWS Organizations to constrain its developers to use only Amazon EC2. Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB. The developers account resides In a dedicated organizational unit (OU). The solutions architect has implemented the following SCP on the developers account:     When this policy is deployed, IAM users in the developers account are still able to use AWS services that are not listed in the policy.  What should the solutions architect do to eliminate the developers' ability to use services outside the scope of this policy?",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Create an explicit deny statement for each AWS service that should be constrained"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Remove the Full AWS Access SCP from the developer account's OU"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Modify the Full AWS Access SCP to explicitly deny all services"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Add an explicit deny statement using a wildcard to the end of the SCP"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "B",
   "explanation": "SCPs never grant permissions, they only cap them, and the default FullAWSAccess SCP still allows every service. An allow-list strategy attaches the narrow EC2/S3/DynamoDB SCP and removes FullAWSAccess so anything unlisted is implicitly denied. A (a deny per service) is unmanageable and misses new services, C (deny everything) blocks even the permitted services, and D (wildcard deny) overrides the allows and also blocks everything.",
   "reference": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html",
   "images": [],
   "interactive": null,
