   "id": "sapc02-q278",
   "number": 166,
   "topic": "Scale-in protection for long-running SQS workers",
   "moduleId": "continuous-improvement",
   "question": "A company is processing videos in the AWS Cloud by using Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group. It takes 30 minutes to process a video. Several EC2 instances scale in and out depending on the number of videos in an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. \fThe company has configured the SQS queue with a redrive policy that specifies a target dead-letter queue and a maxReceiveCount of 1. The company has set the visibility timeout for the SQS queue to 1 hour. The company has set up an Amazon CloudWatch alarm to notify the development team when there are messages in the dead-letter queue.  Several times during the day, the development team receives notification that messages are in the dead-letter queue and that videos have not been processed properly. An investigation finds no errors in the application logs.  How can the company solve this problem?",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Turn on termination protection for the EC2 instances."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Update the visibility timeout for the SOS queue to 3 hours."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Configure scale-in protection for the instances during processing."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Update the redrive policy and set maxReceiveCount to 0."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "C",
   "explanation": "Visibility timeout (1 hour) already exceeds processing time, so a message reappears and hits the DLQ at maxReceiveCount 1 only if the worker disappears mid-job, which scale-in does. Scale-in protection while processing prevents that. Termination protection does not stop ASG scale-in, a longer timeout does not help when the worker is gone, and maxReceiveCount 0 is invalid.",
   "reference": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/as-using-sqs-queue.html",
   "images": [],
   "interactive": null,
   "verified": 2,
   "imported": true,
   "auditVerdict": "verified",
   "auditLabel": "Verified against AWS documentation",
