   "topic": "Replace chained Lambda invocations with Step Functions",
   "moduleId": "continuous-improvement",
   "question": "A retail business runs an orders and payments solution that uses more than 100 AWS Lambda functions that are written in Python. As the company's product catalog grows, the company observes that many of the functions time out. The company reconfigures the functions to have the maximum available timeout values, and the company increases memory for the functions. However, the functions continue to time out.  The company investigates the timeout issue and finds that most failures occur when several functions have been chained together, which results in the initial function timing out while it waits for responses. A solutions architect must resolve these timeout issues while maintaining the cost and operational benefits of using Lambda functions.  Which solution will meet these requirements with the MINIMUM amount of application changes?",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Convert the solution to run in containers. Deploy an Amazon EKS cluster and enable auto scaling for the containers."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Rewrite the Lambda functions in Java. Implement Amazon SQS queues between functions."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Convert the solution to use an AWS Step Functions workflow to invoke the Lambda functions. Remove the direct function-to-function invocations."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Combine groups of linked functions together in a single container image. Deploy the container by using Amazon ECS on Amazon EC2 Spot Instances."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "C",
   "explanation": "Direct Lambda-to-Lambda chaining makes the caller wait and pay while hitting its timeout; Step Functions orchestrates the same Python functions externally, with retries and long-running support, needing only the glue changes. EKS, a Java rewrite with SQS, or ECS on Spot all require far larger rewrites and lose Lambda's operational benefits.",
