   "topic": "Move WAF to CloudFront to cut global latency and cost",
   "moduleId": "continuous-improvement",
   "question": "A company hosts an application behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in the us-east-1 Region. The company serves users from around the world. The company recently attached an AWS WAF web ACL that applies Bot Control and regular-expression rules directly to the ALB.  Amazon CloudWatch shows substantial AWS WAF rule-processing time. During peak usage periods, the ALB TargetResponseTime metric is significantly higher than normal. Users outside North America experience noticeably slower page loads than users in North America. The company wants to reduce global latency while keeping the infrastructure components in a single AWS Region.  Which solution will meet these requirements in the MOST cost-effective way?",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the ALB. Associate the web ACL with the CloudFront distribution and remove the web ACL from the ALB. Modify the ALB to accept connections only from CloudFront."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Increase the ALB capacity units and enable HTTP/2 in us-east-1."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the ALB. Associate the web ACL with the CloudFront distribution. Maintain the web ACL association with the ALB. Modify the ALB to accept connections only from CloudFront."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Deploy additional ALBs in additional Regions. Attach a web ACL to each ALB. Configure latency-based routing."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "A",
   "explanation": "CloudFront serves users from edge locations and runs the web ACL at the edge, so Bot Control and regex inspection happen before traffic reaches the ALB. Removing the web ACL from the ALB avoids paying and waiting for a second WAF evaluation, and restricting the ALB to CloudFront stops bypass. C keeps the duplicate ACL (extra cost and processing), B only adds capacity, and D adds Regions, which conflicts with the single-Region constraint.",
