   "topic": "Detecting and auto-remediating public S3 objects with CloudTrail data events and events rules",
   "moduleId": "continuous-improvement",
   "question": "A Company had a security event whereby an Amazon S3 bucket with sensitive information was made public. Company policy is to never have public S3 objects, and the Compliance team must be informed immediately when any public objects are identified. \fHow can the presence of a public S3 object be detected, set to trigger alarm notifications, and automatically remediated in the future? (Choose two.)",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Turn on object-level logging for Amazon S3. Turn on Amazon S3 event notifications to notify by using an Amazon SNS topic when a PutObject API call is made with a public-read permission."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Configure an Amazon CloudWatch Events rule that invokes an AWS Lambda function to secure the S3 bucket."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Use the S3 bucket permissions for AWS Trusted Advisor and configure a CloudWatch event to notify by using Amazon SNS."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Turn on object-level logging for Amazon S3. Configure a CloudWatch event to notify by using an SNS topic when a PutObject API call with public-read permission is detected in the AWS CloudTrail logs."
    },
    {
     "key": "E",
     "text": "Schedule a recursive Lambda function to regularly change all object permissions inside the S3 bucket."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "BD",
   "explanation": "CloudTrail S3 data events record PutObject/PutObjectAcl calls, which an events rule can match to notify through SNS, and a separate rule can trigger Lambda to remediate. S3 event notifications do not describe ACL grants, Trusted Advisor checks are not an immediate event-driven alert, and periodically rewriting all permissions is blunt and not detection.",
