   "id": "sapc02-q678",
   "number": 516,
   "topic": "Lambda in a VPC needs NAT for internet egress",
   "moduleId": "continuous-improvement",
   "question": "A company is migrating a subset of its application APIs from Amazon EC2 instances to run on a serverless infrastructure. The company has set up Amazon API Gateway, AWS Lambda, and Amazon DynamoDB for the new application. The primary responsibility of the Lambda function is to obtain data from a third-party Software as a Service (SaaS) provider. For consistency, the Lambda function is attached to the same virtual private cloud (VPC) as the original EC2 instances.  Test users report an inability to use this newly moved functionality, and the company is receiving 5xx errors from API Gateway. Monitoring reports from the SaaS provider shows that the requests never made it to its systems. The company notices that Amazon CloudWatch Logs are being generated by the Lambda functions. When the same functionality is tested against the EC2 systems, it works as expected.  What is causing the issue?",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Lambda is in a subnet that does not have a NAT gateway attached to it to connect to the SaaS provider."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "The end-user application is misconfigured to continue using the endpoint backed by EC2 instances."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "The throttle limit set on API Gateway is too low and the requests are not making their way through."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "API Gateway does not have the necessary permissions to invoke Lambda."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "A",
   "explanation": "A VPC-attached Lambda function has no internet path unless private subnets route to a NAT gateway in a public subnet, so calls to the SaaS never leave. Logs prove API Gateway invoked Lambda, ruling out D, and the failing path is the new function, ruling out B; throttling would give 429, not requests silently missing the SaaS.",
   "reference": "https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/internet-access-lambda-function",
   "images": [],
   "interactive": null,
   "verified": 2,
   "imported": true,
   "auditVerdict": "verified",
   "auditLabel": "Verified against AWS documentation",
   "auditNote": "",
   "qtype": "mcq",
   "proof": {
    "accessed": "2026-10-31",
    "claims": [
     {
      "scope": "summary",
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "VPC-connected Lambda needs a NAT gateway to reach the internet.",
      "evidence": [
       {
        "title": "How do I give internet access to a Lambda function that's connected to Amazon VPC?",
        "heading": "Short description",
        "quote": "Without an internet path, a VPC connected function can only connect to resources inside the VPC.",
        "url": "https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/internet-access-lambda-function",
        "accessed": "2026-10-31",
        "applies": "VPC-connected Lambda needs a NAT gateway to reach the internet."
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "scope": "summary",
      "kind": "reasoning",
      "text": "CloudWatch Logs being written shows Lambda was invoked, so permissions and routing from API Gateway work; throttling returns 429 rather than a 5xx from a failed backend call.",
      "evidence": []
     }
    ]
   }
