   "topic": "Resource control policies for org-wide ECR access",
   "moduleId": "org-complexity",
   "question": "A company uses a single organization in AWS Organizations with all features enabled to manage 300 AWS accounts. A solutions architect must ensure that all private Amazon ECR repositories that are deployed in the member accounts are accessible only to principals that belong to the same organization. The company must enforce access controls across the entire organization and for all current and future ECR repositories.  Which solution will meet these requirements?",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Attach an SCP to the organization root that denies all ecr:* actions unless the aws:PrincipalType property has a value of Service."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Attach a resource control policy (RCP) to the organization root that allows access to ECR only when the aws:PrincipalOrgID condition matches the company's organization ID."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Modify IAM policies across all accounts to include a deny statement for ecr:* unless the caller's aws: PrincipalAccount property matches the organization's ID."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Share all ECR repositories through AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM). Control principal access based on account-level allowlists."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "B",
   "explanation": "RCPs set maximum permissions on resources across member accounts, support ECR, and can enforce aws:PrincipalOrgID, covering current and future repositories. An SCP governs principals not resources and cannot do this by PrincipalType; per-account IAM edits and RAM allowlists do not enforce organization-wide, and PrincipalAccount is not an org ID.",
   "reference": "https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/iam-explicit-deny-errors",
