   "moduleId": "new-solutions",
   "question": "A company runs a containerized application that makes predictions based on client messages that arrive in near real time. The number of messages that the application processes varies from a few messages each minute to as many as 200,000 messages each minute. The company needs a solution that scales the application to meet the variable message demand. The solution must also retain a copy of every message for 48 hours for auditing.  Which solution will meet these requirements?",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Create an Amazon SNS topic for incoming messages. Subscribe two Amazon SQS queues to the SNS topic. Configure one queue for application processing and configure the other queue with a 48-hour message retention period for auditing. Deploy the application on Amazon EKS. Configure the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and Cluster Autoscaler to scale based on the processing SQS queue backlog by using external metrics."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Create an Amazon SNS topic for the messages. Deploy the application on Amazon EKS. Configure the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and Cluster Autoscaler to scale directly based on SNS topic metrics. Subscribe an Amazon SQS queue to the SNS topic with a 48-hour retention period to retain processed messages."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Create an Amazon SQS queue for the messages. Deploy the application on Amazon ECS and configure scheduled scaling. Create a second SQS queue with a 48-hour retention period to retain processed messages."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Create an Amazon SNS topic for the messages. Deploy the application on Amazon ECS. Configure ECS Service Auto Scaling to scale based on Amazon CloudWatch metrics. Configure SNS message filtering to route messages directly to ECS tasks. Subscribe an Amazon SQS queue to the SNS topic with a 48-hour retention period for auditing."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "A",
   "explanation": "SNS fans out each message to two SQS queues: one buffers work for the app, the other retains an audit copy (SQS retention goes up to 14 days, so 48 hours fits). On EKS, HPA can scale pods from the SQS backlog through external metrics, with Cluster Autoscaler adding nodes. B scales on SNS metrics (no backlog) and keeps only processed messages, C uses scheduled scaling that cannot track bursts, and D wrongly assumes SNS routes straight to ECS tasks.",
