   "id": "sapc02-q919",
   "number": 779,
   "topic": "Absorbing IoT bursts: DynamoDB capacity and Kinesis batching",
   "moduleId": "new-solutions",
   "question": "A utility company wants to collect usage data every 5 minutes from its smart meters to facilitate time-of-use metering. When a meter sends data to AWS, the data is sent to Amazon API Gateway, processed by an AWS Lambda function. and stored in an Amazon DynamoDB table. During the pilot phase, the Lambda functions took from 3 to 5 seconds to complete.  As more smart meters are deployed, the engineers notice the Lambda functions are taking from 1 to 2 minutes to complete. The functions are also increasing in duration as new types of metrics are collected from the devices. There are many ProvisionedThroughputExceededException errors while performing PUT operations on DynamoDB, and there are also many TooManyRequestsException errors from Lambda.  Which combination of changes will resolve these issues? (Choose two.)",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "text": "Increase the write capacity units to the DynamoDB table."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "text": "Increase the memory available to the Lambda functions."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "text": "Increase the payload size from the smart meters to send more data."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "text": "Stream the data into an Amazon Kinesis data stream from API Gateway and process the data in batches."
    },
    {
     "key": "E",
     "text": "Collect data in an Amazon SQS FIFO queue, which triggers a Lambda function to process each message"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "AD",
   "explanation": "WCU exhaustion causes the DynamoDB throttling, so raising write capacity fixes it. Putting a Kinesis stream between API Gateway and Lambda lets Lambda read records in batches with concurrency bounded by shard count, which relieves the Lambda 429s. More memory does not address throttling, larger payloads lengthen duration, and per-message SQS FIFO invocation keeps invocation volume high.",
   "reference": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/enhanced-throttling-observability-in-amazon-dynamodb/",
   "images": [],
   "interactive": null,
   "verified": 2,
   "imported": true,
   "auditVerdict": "verified",
   "auditLabel": "Verified against AWS documentation",
   "auditNote": "Stem is the classic exam item; no exhibit needed.",
   "qtype": "multi",
   "proof": {
    "accessed": "2026-10-31",
    "claims": [
     {
      "scope": "summary",
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "ProvisionedThroughputExceededException occurs when request traffic exceeds provisioned write capacity, so raising WCU addresses option A.",
      "evidence": [
       {
        "title": "Enhanced throttling observability in Amazon DynamoDB",
        "heading": "Provisioned throughput exceeded exception",
        "quote": "throttling can occur when request traffic exceeds the provisioned read or write capacity for the table or index",
        "url": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/enhanced-throttling-observability-in-amazon-dynamodb/",
        "accessed": "2026-10-31",
        "applies": "ProvisionedThroughputExceededException occurs when request traffic exceeds provisioned write capacity, so raising WCU addresses option A."
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "scope": "summary",
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "Lambda TooManyRequestsException is a 429 throttle caused by requests arriving faster than scaling or exceeding the concurrency limit.",
      "evidence": [
       {
        "title": "How do I troubleshoot Lambda function failures?",
        "heading": "Troubleshoot throttling errors",
        "quote": "If requests to invoke your function arrive faster than it can scale or exceed your concurrency limit, then requests fail with a 429 throttling error.",
        "url": "https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/lambda-troubleshoot-function-failures",
        "accessed": "2026-10-31",
        "applies": "Lambda TooManyRequestsException is a 429 throttle caused by requests arriving faster than scaling or exceeding the concurrency limit."
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "scope": "summary",
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "With Kinesis, Lambda concurrency equals shards times concurrent batches per shard, and records are delivered in batches, so concurrency is bounded.",
      "evidence": [
       {
        "title": "How do I decrease my Lambda functions IteratorAge metric?",
        "heading": "Increase your stream processing throughput",
        "quote": "concurrent runs = number of shards x concurrent batches per shard.",
        "url": "https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/lambda-iterator-age",
        "accessed": "2026-10-31",
        "applies": "With Kinesis, Lambda concurrency equals shards times concurrent batches per shard, and records are delivered in batches, so concurrency is bounded."
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "scope": "summary",
      "kind": "reasoning",
      "text": "B adds memory but does not reduce invocation count or WCU use; C increases work per invocation; E still invokes per message so concurrency stays high.",
      "evidence": []
     }
    ]
   }
